  "issue_title": "[bug] Convergence DoD/degenerate flag reads the monotone best-so-far, so a collapsed policy passes",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\nThe convergence report's degenerate-run flag and the Definition-of-Done verdict `dod_drives_J_upward` are computed on the **best-so-far** curve, which is monotone non-decreasing by construction \u2014 so they can never detect the \"converged to a bad policy\" collapse the module was built to catch. Meanwhile `signal_drop`, the one metric that measures the per-iteration collapse, is computed but never used in any verdict (or even shown in `render()`).\n\n`src/trinity/analysis/convergence.py`:\n\n```python\nbest, signal = _curves(history)          # best = best_fitness (monotone), signal = gen_mean_fitness (J)\n...\nnet_gain = final - initial               # best[-1] - best[0]  >= 0 for essentially every run\nimproved = net_gain > tol\n...\nsignal_drop = (max(signal) - signal[-1]) if signal else 0.0   # computed...\nreturn RunConvergence(..., signal_drop=signal_drop, degenerate=not improved, ...)   # ...but degenerate ignores it\n```\n\n`sep_cmaes.run` writes `best_fitness = opt.best()` each generation \u2014 the best-so-far incumbent, which never falls (the module's own smoke test asserts this monotonicity). So `net_gain >= 0`, `improved` is ~always `True`, `degenerate = not improved` is ~always `False`, and `dod_drives_J_upward = all(r.improved)` is a near-tautology.\n\nBut the module's stated purpose (docstring) is to flag *\"sep-CMA-ES occasionally converged to a bad policy\"*, and the SPEC DoD it checks is *\"the optimizer drives J(\u03b8) upward over iterations\"* where `J(\u03b8) = E[R(\u03c4)]` is the **per-iteration** objective \u2014 the `signal` curve, which genuinely can decline.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n\n*Expected:* a run whose per-iteration objective J collapses is flagged degenerate and fails the DoD.\n\n*Actual:* it passes both, because only the monotone best-so-far is inspected.\n\n**To reproduce** (offline, no GPU/network):\n\n```python\nfrom trinity.analysis.convergence import analyze_run, analyze_runs\n\n# J collapses 0.5 -> 0.4 -> 0.3, but one lucky early gen lifts best-so-far to 0.6.\nhist = [\n    {\"generation\": 0, \"gen_mean_fitness\": 0.5, \"best_fitness\": 0.5},\n    {\"generation\": 1, \"gen_mean_fitness\": 0.4, \"best_fitness\": 0.6},\n    {\"generation\": 2, \"gen_mean_fitness\": 0.3, \"best_fitness\": 0.6},\n]\nr = analyze_run({\"trainer\": \"sep_cmaes\", \"benchmark\": \"math500\"}, hist)\nprint(r.improved, r.degenerate, r.signal_drop)   # True False 0.2   <- collapse missed\nprint(analyze_runs([r])[\"dod_drives_J_upward\"])   # True            <- DoD falsely HOLDS\nprint(analyze_runs([r])[\"degenerate_runs\"])       # []\n```\n\n**Why it matters**\n\nThis module exists to verify the SPEC replication Definition-of-Done and to catch the documented noisy-reward failure where CMA-ES lands on a bad policy. As written it will report \"\u2705 DoD HOLDS\" and no degenerate runs even when every run's actual objective collapsed \u2014 a false green on the exact check it was written for.\n\n**Proposed fix**\n\nRead the collapse/DoD off the per-iteration `signal` curve (already extracted), not the monotone best-so-far. A sign-based criterion avoids arbitrary thresholds:\n\n```python\nsignal_regressed = bool(signal) and signal[-1] < signal[0] - tol\ndegenerate = (not improved) or signal_regressed\n# and DoD requires no collapse:\n\"dod_drives_J_upward\": bool(dod_runs) and all(r.improved and not r.degenerate for r in dod_runs)\n```\n\nSo a run whose J ends below where it started is flagged, while noisy-but-net-upward J is not. Add offline tests for the collapse case, the DoD break, and the noisy-but-upward non-case. Happy to open a PR.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: 3.11\n- GPU: CPU only (bug reproduces offline)\n- Commit hash: `bd65bff`\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-12T19:38:55.000Z",
